A template lets you write text around values. Start its body with a colon.
Inside the body, put a value in brackets where you want its text to appear.

[codeblock, $code "moth":
    name = "Priya"
    greeting = [:Hello, [name]!]
]

The outer brackets build a String. The inner brackets read `name` from the
surrounding scope, so `greeting` contains `Hello, Priya!`.

The part before the colon is the head. Put a formatter there when the text
needs one. This formatter turns a Markdown heading into HTML.

[codeblock, $code "moth":
    heading = [$md:# Build report]
]

An ordinary binding stores the result. In an HTML `@entry.moth`, a bare
top-level template instead contributes a compile-time page fragment. Prefix a
top-level template with `~` to produce its body fragment at runtime. See
@../project-structure/#entry-config (Entry metadata and builder slots).

A receiving type can also ask a construction to collect a sequence. For how
to keep each result as an element, see @../collections/#collection-literals
(Collection literals).
